WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.785
Business continuity in operational technology is the ability to maintain or restore

00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:08.300
critical physical operations at an acceptable level after disruption.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.775
The mission may be safe production, electricity delivery, water treatment, transportation,

00:00:12.775 --> 00:00:16.045
environmental control, or another service that depends on people, equipment,

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:18.200
and control systems working together.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.766
Continuity does not always mean keeping every system fully available.

00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:25.170
A site may shift to a safe degraded mode, reduce capacity, isolate a process,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.160
use alternate equipment, or perform a controlled shutdown.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.370
Plans define the minimum acceptable operation and the conditions for returning to normal.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:37.320
IT service continuity often focuses on restoring applications, data, and user access.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.050
OT continuity includes those concerns but also considers process state,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:45.440
equipment sequencing, utility dependencies, operator visibility, product quality,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.955
environmental consequence, and the safety of shutdown and restart.

00:00:48.955 --> 00:00:53.615
Disaster recovery and incident response support continuity but are not identical to it.

00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:55.462
Recovery restores technology and data.

00:00:55.462 --> 00:00:57.640
Incident response contains and investigates an event.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.640
Continuity coordinates the broader operational choices that protect the mission while

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:02.960
those activities occur.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.519
Safety comes before production pressure.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.920
A fast technical recovery is not successful if process conditions, logic integrity,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.755
alarms, interlocks, or field equipment have not been validated.

00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:15.840
Operations and engineering need authority to decide when restoration is safe.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.750
A useful continuity plan connects critical processes to assets, people, vendors, spares,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.880
utilities, communications, backups, fallback procedures, and tested recovery sequences.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.880
It prepares the organization to make deliberate decisions when normal automation and

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.190
information are unavailable.
